course: dictionary
lesson: 691
title: Bandage: wrapping and protecting
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bandage is a strip of cloth or soft material. You wrap it around a part of the body to cover and protect a wound, or to support an injury. Bandage can be a noun (a bandage) or a verb (to bandage someone), and the context tells you which.
explain: Read this: «For a small cut or graze, wash it with clean water, press with a clean cloth to stop bleeding and cover it with a plaster or bandage.» The order of the steps gives clues: wash, press, then cover. A bandage is the thing you cover the cut with.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «cover it with a strip of cloth or a plaster» still make sense? It does, so your meaning is right.
fact: A bandage is a strip of cloth or soft material wrapped around a part of the body.
fact: «Bandage» can be a noun (a bandage, bandages) or a verb (to bandage, bandaged, bandaging).
fact: «Bandaged» means wrapped in a bandage, as in «bandaged him» in the story of the Samaritan.
fact: A bandage should be firm but not so tight that it stops blood flowing. Check that fingers or toes stay warm and pink.
fact: A first aid kit usually has bandages and plasters, and a bandage can keep a wound clean and hold a dressing in place.
exercise: «For a small cut or graze, wash it with clean water, press with a clean cloth to stop bleeding and cover it with a plaster or bandage.» What is a bandage? | *A strip of cloth or soft material wrapped around a wound | A cold drink for a sore throat | A type of sticking-plaster tin | A sharp tool for cutting
exercise: Ruby says, «She bandaged his knee.» What does bandaged mean? | Kissed his knee better | Washed his trousers | *Wrapped a strip of cloth around his knee | Cut his knee open
exercise: Why must a bandage be firm but not too tight? | So it looks neat and fancy | *So it does not stop blood flowing to the fingers or toes | So it can be used as a rope | So it stays dry in the rain
test: In «When a lawyer asked who his neighbour was, Jesus told of a man robbed and left half dead. A Samaritan stopped, bandaged him and paid for his care», which word shows he wrapped the man's wound? | stopped | care | paid | *bandaged
test: After you bandage an arm, what should you check? | *That the fingers stay warm and pink | That the bandage matches the colour of the sky | That the bandage is as tight as possible | That the arm can be left to swing freely
exam: Which sentence uses bandage as a verb, meaning to wrap? | The bandage was blue and clean | The nurse bought a new bandage | The bandage is in the first aid kit | *The nurse will bandage his ankle
